  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nUpper troughing will continue over the NE CONUS, with ridging from \nTexas NNE into the western Great Lakes region, through late Monday. \nThis will keep dry conditions, with low afternoon RH and \ntemperatures around or slightly below normal, in place across the \nforecast area. Speaking of low RH values, the NBM is once again too \nhigh with afternoon dewpoints, so a heavy handed dose of CONSShort \nand HRRR dewpoints were mixed in for this afternoon and Monday as \nwell. By Tuesday the upper trough pulls away into the northern \nAtlantic, and the ridge shifts east towards the Appalachian, \nremaining there through the latter part of the week. Expect \ncontinued dry conditions, with a notable warm up Tuesday through \nThursday, with afternoon temperatures rising several degrees above \nnormal.\n\nFor the end of the week, there's mixed reviews on how the pattern \nevolves and how quickly rain chances return. The evolution of the \npattern across the CONUS will be based on the interaction of an \nupper low that will move onshore in southern California and \nHurricane Polo which is forecast to move NNE across the Baja \npeninsula, both of which occur on Monday. Previous model runs had \nthose phasing into a closed upper over the desert southwest and more \nslowly shifting east across the southern CONUS through the week, \nwith a weaker northern stream jet across southern Canada, yielding \nprolonged ridging over the southeast and a late Friday or maybe \nSaturday return of rain chances to our area. Trends in some guidance \nover the last 24 hours have been for a stronger Canadian jet, a \nquicker eastward movement of the upper low and subsequent weakening \nof the southeast ridge, and thus a quicker return of Gulf moisture \nand rain chances to the southern Appalachian. That's not universally \nthe case though, as some guidance maintains a stronger ridge and \nslower progression of things, still putting rain off until late \nSaturday into Sunday. Suffice it to say, the forecast for Friday \ninto next weekend is far from certain and there's plenty of time for \nsignificant shifts in the forecast. For right now, the forecast will \nreflect continued above normal temperatures for both Friday and \nSaturday (although closer to normal for Sat), with rain chances \nreturning Friday afternoon and continuing into Saturday. I wouldn't \nbe at all surprised if the slower timing is correct and the Friday \nrain forecast winds up being too pessimistic, but it's also not an \nunreasonable forecast for the moment either.",
